<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euthymius of Tarnovo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1325–c. 1402</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euthymius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> was the last patriarch of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, from 1375 until the Ottoman capture of Tarnovo in 1393. A Hesychast monk and pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-of-tarnovo">Theodosius of Tarnovo</a>, he reformed the language and spelling of Church Slavonic books and wrote lives of saints. His Tarnovo literary school influenced Serbian, Romanian and Russian letters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euthymius-of-tarnovo.jpg" alt="Euthymius of Tarnovo" width="297" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stolichanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euthymius_of_Tarnovo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
